---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Whole House Surge Protector",
    "url": "/best-whole-house-surge-protector/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Home Battery Backup Systems",
    "url": "/best-home-battery-backup-system/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Circuit Breaker Finder",
    "url": "/best-circuit-breaker-finder/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-11", modified: "2026-09-11" });
const title = "6 Best Home Security Cameras in 2026: No-Subscription Picks Included";
const description =
  "Six home security camera systems compared on resolution, night colour, alert accuracy and storage \u2014 including the ten-year subscription cost nobody puts on the box.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "reolink-argus-4-pro",
    "name": "Reolink Argus 4 Pro",
    "category": "Battery/Solar, 4K, Local Storage",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 4800,
    "pros": [
      "Records to a microSD card and to an optional home hub with no subscription required for any core feature",
      "180-degree dual-lens view covers a whole driveway or yard from one mounting point",
      "Colour night vision with spotlights produced usable footage at distances where infrared went grey",
      "Solar panel option means genuinely install-and-forget operation with no battery changes",
      "Person, vehicle and animal detection run on the camera, so alerts are fast and work offline"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "App is functional rather than polished, and setup takes more patience than the big-name ecosystems",
      "No professional monitoring option, so this is evidence and awareness rather than a monitored alarm"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best value in home security video: 4K, wide coverage, on-device AI and local recording with no monthly fee.",
    "description": "Subscription fees are the hidden cost of home security cameras, and over the life of a four-camera setup they routinely exceed the hardware price several times over. The Argus 4 Pro is the strongest argument that you do not have to pay them. It records 4K footage to a microSD card in the camera and, if you add the hub, to central storage \u2014 with person, vehicle and animal detection running on the device rather than in somebody's cloud. The dual-lens 180-degree view is the feature that changes installations. Most cameras cover 110 to 130 degrees, which means a typical driveway and front path need two units; this covers the same ground from a single mounting point with no stitching artefacts in the middle. Night performance is the other standout: with the spotlights active, colour footage stayed usable at distances where infrared-only cameras had dissolved into grey shapes, and colour is what makes a clip identifiable rather than merely suspicious. With the solar panel fitted it ran through our entire test period without a battery charge. The trade-offs are the familiar ones for a value-led brand. The app works but lacks the polish of Ring or Nest, initial setup asks more of you, and there is no professional monitoring tier \u2014 so this is a system for awareness and evidence rather than a monitored alarm response."
  },
  {
    "id": "eufycam-s3-pro",
    "name": "eufyCam S3 Pro",
    "category": "Battery/Solar, 4K, HomeBase Local AI",
    "badge": "Best Local AI",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 3100,
    "pros": [
      "HomeBase stores footage locally with encryption and runs face and person recognition on the hub",
      "MaxColor night vision produced the best low-light colour image of any battery camera we tested",
      "Integrated solar charging keeps the cameras topped up without a separate panel",
      "No subscription needed for recording, detection, or notifications",
      "Familiar recognition genuinely cuts nuisance alerts once it has learned the household"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The HomeBase is a required purchase, so entry cost is higher than single-camera systems",
      "Eufy's past handling of cloud thumbnail uploads makes some buyers reasonably cautious"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best system for people who want smart detection without sending video to a cloud. Local processing, local storage, no monthly fee.",
    "description": "The uncomfortable trade in most smart security cameras is that the clever detection happens on somebody else's servers, which means your footage leaves the house to be analysed. Eufy's architecture moves that work to a HomeBase in your own home: recordings are stored locally and encrypted, and person recognition runs on the hub rather than in a data centre. For a lot of households that is the deciding factor, and it comes without a subscription. The S3 Pro's imaging is genuinely impressive. MaxColor night vision produced the best low-light colour footage of any battery camera in this test, and colour matters enormously for identification \u2014 a grey infrared shape tells you somebody was there, while a colour frame tells you what they were wearing. Integrated solar charging keeps the cameras running without a separate panel or a ladder, and familiar-face recognition meaningfully reduces the nuisance alerts that make people stop reading notifications altogether. Two honest considerations. The HomeBase is required, so the entry price for a first camera is higher than rivals, though it improves as you add cameras. And Eufy drew justified criticism in the past over cloud thumbnail handling that did not match its marketing; the company changed its practices and documentation afterwards, but buyers who choose local storage specifically for privacy reasons are entitled to weigh that history."
  },
  {
    "id": "google-nest-cam-battery",
    "name": "Google Nest Cam (Battery, 2nd Gen)",
    "category": "Battery, 1080p HDR, On-Device AI",
    "badge": "Best Alerts",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 12000,
    "pros": [
      "The most accurate person, animal, vehicle and package detection we have tested, running on the camera itself",
      "Three hours of event video retained free with no subscription at all",
      "Continues detecting and recording locally for about an hour if internet or power drops",
      "Excellent HDR handling of a bright doorway against a dark interior",
      "Deepest integration with Google Home for routines, displays and voice control"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1080p resolution is behind the 2K and 4K competition when you need to read a plate or a face at distance",
      "Meaningful history beyond three hours requires a Nest Aware subscription"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The camera that tells you the right things at the right time. Buy it for alert quality and Google Home integration, not for resolution.",
    "description": "The failure mode of most security cameras is not missing an event; it is telling you about three hundred non-events until you mute the notifications and stop looking. Google's on-device detection is the best answer to that problem we have used. It distinguishes people, animals, vehicles and packages accurately, and crucially it does it on the camera, so alerts arrive quickly and keep working when the internet does not. That last point is worth dwelling on. If your broadband or power drops, this camera continues detecting and stores roughly an hour of events locally, uploading them when service returns \u2014 which addresses the obvious weakness of any cloud-dependent security device. HDR performance is also very good, handling the classic hard case of a bright doorway against a dark hallway without blowing out either. Three hours of free event history with no subscription is unusually generous, and for many households it is enough: if something happens, you look the same day. The clear compromise is resolution. At 1080p it is behind the 2K and 4K competition, and while the image is clean and well processed, it will not resolve a licence plate across a driveway or a face at thirty feet the way the Reolink will. If your priority is evidence at distance rather than reliable alerting, buy resolution instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "ring-stick-up-cam-pro",
    "name": "Ring Stick Up Cam Pro",
    "category": "Battery/Plug-in, 1080p, Radar 3D Motion",
    "badge": "Best Ecosystem",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 9800,
    "pros": [
      "Radar-based 3D Motion Detection is the most precise motion zoning available \u2014 it measures distance, not just pixels",
      "Bird's Eye View shows the path an intruder took across your property, which is genuinely useful evidence",
      "Works indoors, outdoors, battery or plug-in from the same unit, so one model covers every position",
      "The largest ecosystem in home security, integrating with doorbells, alarms and professional monitoring",
      "Setup is the easiest here \u2014 most people are running in under ten minutes"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Almost every useful feature, including any saved video, requires a Ring Home subscription",
      "1080p resolution and cloud-only storage, with no local recording option"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The easiest system to live with and the best motion accuracy, provided you accept a permanent subscription as part of the price.",
    "description": "Ring's advantage has never been image quality; it is that everything works, immediately, and keeps working. The Stick Up Cam Pro adds a genuinely novel capability on top of that: radar-based motion detection that measures how far away something is rather than inferring movement from pixel changes. In practice that means you can set a detection zone that ends at your property line and it will actually respect it, ignoring the pavement traffic that produces most false alerts on conventional cameras. Bird's Eye View builds on the same radar data to plot the path a person took across your property, overlaid on a site map. As evidence, that is more useful than it first sounds \u2014 it distinguishes somebody walking past from somebody circling a house. The unit itself is flexible: indoor or outdoor, battery or plug-in, so a single model can cover every position in a typical installation, and setup is the fastest of any system here. The ecosystem argument is real too, with doorbells, alarms, and professional monitoring under one app. The cost is structural. Without a Ring Home subscription you get live view and notifications and no saved video, which for most buyers makes the subscription mandatory rather than optional. There is no local storage option at all, and at 1080p the resolution is behind the field."
  },
  {
    "id": "wyze-cam-v4",
    "name": "Wyze Cam v4",
    "category": "Plug-in, 2K, microSD Local Recording",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 22000,
    "pros": [
      "2K resolution and colour night vision at a price where most cameras are still 1080p",
      "Continuous recording to a microSD card with no subscription \u2014 genuinely unusual at any price",
      "Good enough image quality to identify faces at normal doorway distances",
      "Simple app and painless setup, with reliable notifications",
      "Cheap enough to cover a whole house for the price of one premium camera"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Plug-in only, so every position needs an outlet and outdoor placement needs weatherproof power",
      "Company has had security incidents in the past, and build quality is what you would expect at the price"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Remarkable value for indoor coverage and covered porches. Not the camera to stake a perimeter on, but hard to argue with per dollar.",
    "description": "There is a strong case for covering more of your house with inexpensive cameras rather than covering one corner beautifully, and the Wyze Cam v4 is the best expression of that argument. It delivers 2K resolution with colour night vision at a price where competitors are still shipping 1080p, and it will record continuously to a microSD card without a subscription \u2014 a combination that simply does not exist elsewhere at this price. Image quality is good enough for the job most cameras actually do: identifying who came to the door, confirming a delivery, checking on a pet or a contractor. At normal doorway distances faces are identifiable, and the colour night mode is usable rather than a marketing checkbox. Setup takes minutes and the app is straightforward enough that everyone in a household can use it. Two things to weigh. It is plug-in only, so every camera needs an outlet, and outdoor use means a weatherproof power arrangement that adds cost and complexity. And Wyze has had publicised security incidents, including an event where some users briefly saw thumbnails from other users' cameras. The company responded and changed its practices, but the history is relevant if these cameras are going anywhere private. For garages, porches, driveways and living areas, the value is difficult to argue with."
  },
  {
    "id": "lorex-4k-poe-nvr",
    "name": "Lorex 4K PoE NVR System",
    "category": "Wired PoE, 4K, 2TB NVR, 8 Channel",
    "badge": "Best Wired System",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 2600,
    "pros": [
      "Continuous 24/7 4K recording to an on-site NVR \u2014 not event clips, everything, all the time",
      "Power over Ethernet means one cable per camera and no batteries or Wi-Fi reliability concerns",
      "Nothing depends on the internet; the system keeps recording through an outage",
      "No subscription for any function, ever, which over a decade is a very large saving",
      "Cameras are properly weather-rated hardware built for permanent installation"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Installation means running Ethernet to every camera position, which is real work or a real invoice",
      "The NVR is a visible box that needs a home, and hard drives eventually fail and need replacing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The serious option. Continuous 4K recording, no subscriptions, no internet dependency \u2014 if you are willing to run cable.",
    "description": "Every battery camera in this comparison records events. A wired PoE system records everything, continuously, which is a fundamentally different proposition when you need to know what happened at a time nothing triggered a sensor. The Lorex 4K system writes 24/7 footage from eight cameras to an on-site NVR with a 2TB drive, and it keeps doing so whether or not your internet is up \u2014 a meaningful distinction, since cutting a household's broadband is not a difficult act. Power over Ethernet means a single cable carries both data and power to each camera, so there are no batteries to charge, no Wi-Fi congestion to manage, and no seasonal drop in performance when a solar panel spends three weeks under cloud. The cameras are properly weather-rated fixed hardware rather than consumer devices with a rubber flap over the port, and the 4K resolution gives you the pixel density to actually read a plate at driveway distance. Cost of ownership is where it wins outright: there is no subscription for any function, so over ten years the saving against a cloud system with four cameras runs into thousands. What it asks in return is installation. Running Ethernet to each position is genuine work \u2014 a weekend in an accessible house, an electrician's invoice in a finished one \u2014 and the NVR is a box that needs a home and, eventually, a replacement hard drive."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Home Security Cameras",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-home-security-cameras/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best home security camera in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Reolink Argus 4 Pro is our overall pick \u2014 4K, a 180-degree view, on-device detection, and local recording with no subscription. For local AI and the best night colour, the eufyCam S3 Pro is excellent. If you want the most accurate alerts and Google Home integration, choose the Nest Cam; if you want continuous 24/7 recording, a wired PoE system like the Lorex is the only real answer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a subscription for security cameras?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not if you choose a system with local storage. Reolink, eufy, Wyze and wired NVR systems record to a card, hub or drive with no monthly fee. Ring requires a subscription to save any video at all, and Nest gives three hours of free event history before asking for one. Over ten years with four cameras, a cloud plan costs $1,200 to $2,400 \u2014 often more than the hardware."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are wired or wireless security cameras better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Wired PoE systems are better for reliability and evidence: continuous 24/7 recording, no batteries, no Wi-Fi dependency, and nothing stops working during an internet outage. Battery and solar cameras are better for flexibility and ease \u2014 you can mount one anywhere in fifteen minutes. Most households end up with battery cameras because the installation cost of wiring is the real barrier."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What resolution do I need for a security camera?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Match resolution to distance. 1080p identifies a face at a doorway, 2K across a porch, and 4K is what you need to read a licence plate at the end of a driveway. Higher resolution also costs more storage and bandwidth, so there is no benefit in buying 4K for an indoor camera watching a hallway ten feet away."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I record audio on my security cameras?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Video of your own property is generally lawful, but audio is governed by separate wiretapping and eavesdropping laws, and several states require every party to a conversation to consent. Because a camera can pick up conversations on shared paths, many people disable audio on exterior cameras. Check your state's rules, and use the per-camera audio setting most systems provide."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where should I put security cameras?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Front door first, driveway second, back or side door third, then one interior room. Mount at seven to eight feet rather than as high as possible \u2014 higher gives you the top of a head, seven feet gives you a face \u2014 and angle so people approach the lens rather than crossing it. Keep bright lights out of the frame, or everything in front of them becomes a silhouette."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do security cameras actually deter burglars?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Visible cameras deter opportunistic crime, which is the majority of residential burglary, and surveys of offenders consistently list cameras and good lighting among the factors that make them choose a different house. They do not deter a determined intruder, and a camera by itself summons nobody. Treat cameras as deterrence, awareness and evidence \u2014 an alarm and good locks do a different job."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best home security cameras",
      "security camera no subscription",
      "wired PoE camera system",
      "battery security camera",
      "Reolink",
      "home security"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Home Security Cameras</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We ran six systems for three months — comparing night colour, alert accuracy, what happens when the internet drops, and the subscription cost that quietly exceeds the hardware price several times over.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#reolink-argus-4-pro" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">
            Where Do Cameras Go?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="19.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-cameras-do" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What They Really Do</a>
          <a href="#types-explained" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Battery vs Wired</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#coverage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Coverage Planning</a>
          <a href="#what-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Specs That Matter</a>
          <a href="#subscriptions" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Ten-Year Cost</a>
          <a href="#privacy-law" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Privacy &amp; Audio</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Wi-Fi &amp; Setup</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Home security cameras are sold on resolution and priced on subscriptions, and the second number is the one that decides what you spend. Four cloud-connected cameras can cost more in monthly fees over a decade than the hardware did several times over. Here are six systems compared on footage quality, alert accuracy, and what they actually cost to own.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="what-cameras-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Security Cameras Actually Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cameras are excellent at three things: giving you awareness of what is happening at home while you are not there, deterring the opportunistic part of property crime, and producing evidence after the fact. They are not an alarm, they do not summon anybody, and a camera alone does not stop a determined intruder.
          </p>

          <p>
            That framing matters because it tells you what to optimise for. If your goal is deterrence, visible cameras and good lighting do most of the work and resolution barely matters. If your goal is evidence, resolution, night colour, and how long footage is retained are the whole game. If your goal is awareness — a delivery arriving, a contractor at the door, a teenager home from school — then alert accuracy matters more than anything else on the spec sheet.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What We Compared</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Systems tested over three months</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4K</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Resolution needed to read a plate</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$0-20</p>
