          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Specs That Decide Useful Footage
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Resolution against distance.</strong> 1080p identifies a face at a doorway. 2K identifies a face across a porch. 4K is what you need to read a licence plate at the end of a driveway. Decide what you need to recognise and how far away it will be, and buy resolution accordingly rather than by default.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Colour night vision.</strong> Infrared tells you somebody was there; colour tells you what they were wearing, which is the difference between a clip that helps and one that does not. Colour night modes need either a spotlight or a very sensitive sensor, and the quality varies enormously between models.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Detection type.</strong> Pixel-change motion detection produces alerts for shadows, rain, headlights, and cats. On-device person, vehicle and package detection cuts that to a fraction. Radar-based zoning, as on the Ring Pro, goes further by measuring actual distance so a zone can stop at your property line.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Where the processing happens.</strong> On-device or on-hub detection produces faster alerts and keeps working when the internet is down. Cloud detection introduces a delay and a dependency, and means video leaves your home to be analysed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retention.</strong> A camera is worthless for anything you discover late if it only keeps three days. Check how long footage is kept on the plan you will actually pay for, and whether local storage is available as a backstop.
          </p>
        </div>

