        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="privacy-law" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Privacy, Neighbours, and Audio
          </h2>

          <p>
            Recording video of your own property, including areas visible from it, is generally lawful across the United States. The three places people get into difficulty are audio, expectation of privacy, and aim.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Audio</strong> is governed by wiretapping and eavesdropping law rather than video law, and several states require all parties to a conversation to consent to its recording. A camera that records a conversation on a shared path or in a back garden can raise issues that the same camera recording silent video would not. Many systems let you disable audio recording per camera, and where you are unsure, that is the conservative setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expectation of privacy</strong> is the second line. Cameras inside a home are your business, with the clear exception of bathrooms and bedrooms occupied by guests, lodgers, or anyone else who reasonably expects privacy there. If you rent out a room or use a short-term letting platform, disclosed exterior cameras are normal and undisclosed interior ones are not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Aim</strong> is the practical one. A camera pointed into a neighbour's garden or through their windows creates a dispute you do not want and, in some jurisdictions, a legal problem. Use privacy zones — most systems support masking part of the frame — to black out what is not yours, and talk to neighbours before mounting something that overlooks their property. Rules vary by state and locality, so check your own if a camera will see beyond your boundary.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Wi-Fi, Installation and Keeping It Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common cause of a camera system that disappoints is not the camera; it is Wi-Fi. Cameras sit at the edge of the house, often behind a wall of brick or foil-backed insulation, and stream continuously. Check signal strength at the mounting position before you drill, and be prepared to add an access point or mesh node — that is a cheaper fix than replacing cameras.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most consumer cameras use the 2.4 GHz band because it travels further, which also means they compete with every other device in the neighbourhood. If several cameras drop out at the same times each day, channel congestion is the likely cause rather than the hardware. A dedicated network or a wired PoE system removes the problem entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is light but not zero. Clean the lens covers a couple of times a year, because spider webs and dust across the lens cause endless false alerts at night when the infrared illuminates them. Check battery levels seasonally on solar cameras, since three weeks of winter cloud can outpace charging. And update firmware — security cameras are internet-connected devices that have historically been a target, and outdated firmware is the most common vulnerability.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, protect the account. Use a unique password and enable two-factor authentication on any camera account, especially one with interior cameras. The incidents that make headlines are almost always credential reuse rather than a breach of the manufacturer, and that is entirely within your control.
          </p>
        </div>


