            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a subscription for security cameras?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if you choose a system with local storage. Reolink, eufy, Wyze and wired NVR systems record to a card, hub or drive with no monthly fee. Ring requires a subscription to save any video at all, and Nest gives three hours of free event history before asking for one. Over ten years with four cameras, a cloud plan costs $1,200 to $2,400 — often more than the hardware.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are wired or wireless security cameras better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wired PoE systems are better for reliability and evidence: continuous 24/7 recording, no batteries, no Wi-Fi dependency, and nothing stops working during an internet outage. Battery and solar cameras are better for flexibility and ease — you can mount one anywhere in fifteen minutes. Most households end up with battery cameras because the installation cost of wiring is the real barrier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What resolution do I need for a security camera?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Match resolution to distance. 1080p identifies a face at a doorway, 2K across a porch, and 4K is what you need to read a licence plate at the end of a driveway. Higher resolution also costs more storage and bandwidth, so there is no benefit in buying 4K for an indoor camera watching a hallway ten feet away.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I record audio on my security cameras?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Video of your own property is generally lawful, but audio is governed by separate wiretapping and eavesdropping laws, and several states require every party to a conversation to consent. Because a camera can pick up conversations on shared paths, many people disable audio on exterior cameras. Check your state's rules, and use the per-camera audio setting most systems provide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put security cameras?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Front door first, driveway second, back or side door third, then one interior room. Mount at seven to eight feet rather than as high as possible — higher gives you the top of a head, seven feet gives you a face — and angle so people approach the lens rather than crossing it. Keep bright lights out of the frame, or everything in front of them becomes a silhouette.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do security cameras actually deter burglars?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Visible cameras deter opportunistic crime, which is the majority of residential burglary, and surveys of offenders consistently list cameras and good lighting among the factors that make them choose a different house. They do not deter a determined intruder, and a camera by itself summons nobody. Treat cameras as deterrence, awareness and evidence — an alarm and good locks do a different job.
              </p>
            </div>

